WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.735
Production queries rarely remain a single
fixed comparison.

2
00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:07.485
Search endpoints add optional status,
owner, date, and text filters;

3
00:00:07.565 --> 00:00:11.795
reports select predefined groupings; bulk
reads accept multiple identifiers;

4
00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:14.363
and pagination introduces limits and
offsets.

5
00:00:14.443 --> 00:00:18.299
Complexity often causes safe code to
regress into string concatenation.

6
00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:20.930
The remedy is to keep two construction
channels:

7
00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.373
trusted code assembles known SQL
fragments,

8
00:00:23.453 --> 00:00:27.497
while every externally controlled value is
appended to a separate parameter

9
00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:28.261
collection.

10
00:00:28.341 --> 00:00:32.757
For optional filters, begin with an array
of clauses authored by the application and

11
00:00:32.837 --> 00:00:33.833
an array of values.

12
00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:35.779
When a validated status is present,

13
00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:39.840
append it to the values and add a fixed
predicate whose placeholder number is

14
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.724
derived from the new array length.

15
00:00:41.804 --> 00:00:46.220
Repeat for each supported filter, then
join only those trusted predicates with AND.

16
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.913
The request value never enters the clause
string.

17
00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:52.663
A query builder can provide the same
separation through method calls,

18
00:00:52.743 --> 00:00:56.786
provided raw expressions are not used to
smuggle text past its binding layer.

19
00:00:56.866 --> 00:00:59.417
Multi-value lookup needs deliberate
handling.

20
00:00:59.497 --> 00:01:03.415
Use a documented array parameter where the
driver and database support it,

21
00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:07.352
or have trusted code generate one
placeholder per value and add each value

22
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:08.116
separately.

23
00:01:08.196 --> 00:01:10.871
An empty list needs an explicit semantic
decision,

24
00:01:10.951 --> 00:01:13.564
such as returning no rows without issuing
a query,

25
00:01:13.644 --> 00:01:17.189
rather than producing invalid SQL or
removing a protective filter.

26
00:01:17.269 --> 00:01:21.624
Bound pagination values should also pass
numeric range checks so an otherwise safe

27
00:01:21.704 --> 00:01:24.503
query cannot request an unreasonable
amount of work.

28
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:29.248
Parameterization protects syntax;
validation protects resource and business limits.

29
00:01:29.328 --> 00:01:33.434
As queries grow, encode each optional
feature as a small trusted function that

30
00:01:33.514 --> 00:01:36.002
contributes a known fragment and bound
values.

31
00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:40.623
Test combinations, because placeholder
numbering, empty conditions, parentheses,

32
00:01:40.703 --> 00:01:44.248
and joins can produce correctness defects
even without injection.

33
00:01:44.328 --> 00:01:46.879
Reporting features deserve special
attention:

34
00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:50.131
selectable columns and groupings are
structural mappings,

35
00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:53.072
while date ranges and search terms remain
parameters.

36
00:01:53.152 --> 00:01:57.569
The consistent test is whether deleting
all parameter values would leave a complete

37
00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:00.510
SQL program assembled solely from trusted
fragments.

38
00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.571
If so, the program structure and its data
have remained separate throughout

39
00:02:04.651 --> 00:02:05.460
construction.
